["Bridging Language Gaps with Adaptive RAG: Improving Indonesian Language Question Answering 
II Related Works
II-D Single and Multi Step Retrieval
However recent studies around retrieval methods has been focused in multi-step retrieval, improving the ability of retrieval system to gather multiple information that is needed for answering specifically complex question [
[16](#bib.bib16)
] [
[17](#bib.bib17)
] . Studies have proposed methods such as SelfAsk [
[50](#bib.bib50)
] prompts an LLM to breakdown question into sub-questions so the prompt can get better by knowing what questions should be asked to get the final answer. Similarly DecomP or decomposed prompting [
[51](#bib.bib51)
] uses LLM to breakdown complex question into a more simpler task that is more manageable to complete. With the introduction of chain-of-thought prompting-where a model is encouraged to explain its reasoning process step by step rather than providing an answer directly-the reasoning performance of LLMs has improved significantly [
[52](#bib.bib52)
] . Building on this idea, several approaches have been proposed to enhance retrieval and reasoning. For example, ReAct [
[53](#bib.bib53)
] encourages LLMs to interleave reasoning with actions in their outputs, although this method tends to work more effectively with larger or fine-tuned models rather than smaller ones. More recently, Adaptive-RAG [
[17](#bib.bib17)
] integrates the state-of-the-art IRCoT [
[16](#bib.bib16)
] for multi-step retrieval, where an interleaving approach guides the retrieval process so that prompts can more effectively provide the context needed for answering questions comprehensively.", "Agent-Orchestrated Adaptive RAG: A Comparative  Study on Structured and Multi-Hop Retrieval
II Background and Related Work
II-D Reasoning and Multi-Hop Retrieval
Several prompting frameworks elicit explicit reasoning in LLMs. Chain-of-Thought prompting [
[48](#bib.bib54)
] encourages the model to generate intermediate reasoning steps before committing to an answer, improving performance on tasks that require composition. Least-to-Most prompting [
[54](#bib.bib50)
] decomposes a hard problem into a sequence of simpler sub-problems solved in order, an approach that aligns closely with the query decomposition strategy adopted in this work. Self-refinement methods iterate on a model's own draft using its self-generated feedback [
[28](#bib.bib52)
] , and inference-time self-correction has been surveyed as a general mechanism for improving reliability [
[20](#bib.bib18)
] .
Multi-hop question answering benchmarks provide the testbed for these methods. HotpotQA [
[52](#bib.bib6)
] popularized explainable multi-hop evaluation, and MuSiQue [
[43](#bib.bib7)
] was deliberately constructed by composing single-hop questions so that answering requires genuine evidence integration across independent documents and resists single-hop shortcuts. Tree- and graph-structured retrieval has been proposed to better capture the dependencies among reasoning steps [
[41](#bib.bib14)
] . Despite this activity, comparatively few studies place a multi-hop benchmark alongside a structured, domain-specific corpus to isolate how the same agentic mechanisms behave under different reasoning demands-the contrast this work is designed to expose."]

["Towards Fair RAG: On the Impact of Fair Ranking in Retrieval-Augmented Generation Conference: Proceedings of the 2025 International ACM SIGIR Conference on Innovative Concepts and Theories in Information Retrieval (ICTIR); July 18, 2025; Padua, Italy Proceedings of the 2025 International ACM SIGIR Conference on Innovative Concepts and Theories in Information Retrieval (ICTIR) (ICTIR '25), July 18, 2025, Padua, Italy DOI: 10.1145/3731120.3744599 ISBN: 979-8-4007-1861-8/2025/07 CCS: Computing methodologies Natural language generation CCS: Information systems Evaluation of retrieval results
1. Introduction
Our empirical results show that, in the context of machine users, there also exists an overall trend of fairness-quality tradeoff with respect to both retrieval and generation quality. However, the magnitude of this tradeoff is not particularly severe. In fact, we find that RAG models equipped with a fair ranker can often preserve a significant level of retrieval and generation quality, and in some cases, even surpass the quality achieved by the traditional RAG setup with a deterministic ranker that lacks fairness considerations. Moreover, while the fraction of retrieved sources that actually appear in the final response may vary, equitable retrieval frequently leads to more equitable usage of those sources by the generator.
This surprising finding offers significant insight into the potential of RAG-based applications, suggesting that fair treatment of individual content providers can be achieved without sacrificing much of the high-quality service delivered to end-users. This challenges the conventional assumption of an inevitable tradeoff between fairness and quality, opening new avenues for developing more equitable and effective RAG systems.", "Towards a Search Engine for Machines: Unified Ranking for Multiple Retrieval-Augmented Large Language Models Conference: Proceedings of the 47th International ACM SIGIR Conference on Research and Development in Information Retrieval; July 14-18, 2024; Washington, DC, USA Proceedings of the 47th International ACM SIGIR Conference on Research and Development in Information Retrieval (SIGIR '24), July 14-18, 2024, Washington, DC, USA DOI: 10.1145/3626772.3657733 ISBN: 979-8-4007-0431-4/24/07 CCS: Computing methodologies Natural language generation CCS: Information systems Information retrieval CCS: Information systems Retrieval models and ranking CCS: Information systems Question answering CCS: Computing methodologies Machine learning
2. Related Work
Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG)
RAG (
[Lewis et al. 2020b](#bib.bib26)
) is a paradigm that integrates information retrieval (IR) and natural language generation (NLG). This approach aims to enhance the quality and relevance of generated content by leveraging external knowledge sources during the generation process (
[Asai et al. 2023](#bib.bib4)
;
[Siriwardhana et al. 2023](#bib.bib45)
) . Unlike traditional large language models that rely solely on knowledge learned during their pre-training, RAG systems dynamically fetch information from a knowledge source using a retriever, allowing them to generate contextually rich and factually accurate outputs (
[Zamani et al. 2022](#bib.bib58)
) . RAG demonstrates versatility with numerous use cases and applications. It finds application in knowledge-grounding within textual (
[Petroni et al. 2021](#bib.bib32)
;
[Lewis et al. 2020b](#bib.bib26)
;
[Izacard and Grave 2021](#bib.bib17)
) or multi-modal (
[Salemi et al. 2023a](#bib.bib37)
;
[Chen et al. 2022](#bib.bib6)
;
[Gui et al. 2022](#bib.bib13)
;
[Salemi et al. 2023b](#bib.bib40)"]

["Retrieval-Augmented Online Learning for Concept Drift Adaptation
2 Related Work
2.1 Online learning and concept drift
Concept drift has long been recognised as a central challenge for inductive learning in dynamic environments [
[1](#bib.bib1)
] . Passive adaptation schemes restrict effective training data to a recent horizon using sliding windows, decaying weights or dynamic ensembles [
[3](#bib.bib3)
,
[4](#bib.bib4)
] . Drift detectors such as ADWIN [
[5](#bib.bib5)
] adjust window sizes based on statistical tests. A comprehensive survey is given by Gama et al. [
[2](#bib.bib2)
] . These approaches work well for smooth, one-way drift but do not explicitly exploit reoccurring regimes: once an old concept has been forgotten, it must be re-learned when it returns.
Non-stationary online learning has also been analysed from a regret perspective. Zinkevich [
[13](#bib.bib13)
] established foundational results for online convex programming. Subsequent work introduced variation budgets and adaptive regret, yielding bounds that scale as O ⁡ ( T + V T ) O(\sqrt{T}+V_{T}) where V T V_{T} measures the total amount of change [
[14](#bib.bib14)
,
[15](#bib.bib15)
] . These analyses typically assume that examples are observed once and then discarded; explicit memory and retrieval mechanisms are rarely considered. Our discussion in Section
[5](#S5)
builds on this line of work.", "Online Learning via Memory:  Retrieval-Augmented Detector Adaptation
1 Introduction
Object detection has advanced significantly over the years. Traditionally, effective object detectors were developed by collecting a large amount of annotated training data and training the model offline, a process that could take days or weeks and result in models tightly coupled to specific datasets or domains, such as Faster-RCNN [
[13](#bib.bib13)
] , YOLO [
[12](#bib.bib12)
] and etc. on COCO dataset [
[5](#bib.bib5)
] . Recently, with advancements in foundational model-accelerated pseudo labeling pipelines and more comprehensive training datasets, object detectors have evolved from closed-set to open-world models. These new models, like GLIP [
[4](#bib.bib4)
] and Grounding-DINO [
[7](#bib.bib7)
] , are capable of detecting wide range of objects.
However, applying these models to novel domains often results in poor detection performance. For example, the state-of-the-art open-set model Grounding-DINO [
[7](#bib.bib7)
] , primarily trained on large-scale natural scene datasets, achieves less than 1% mAP on the aerial dataset DOTA [
[14](#bib.bib14)
] . To adapt to new visual domains and class vocabularies, these models still require offline retraining or fine-tuning. Traditional offline learning paradigms face two major bottlenecks: (1) the need for extensive labeling of target-domain data to enable stable training, and (2) a time-consuming retraining/fine-tuning process. These overheads hinder the efficient adaptation of detector models in real-world scenarios."]

